<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bernhard Rothmann</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1495–c. 1535</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bernhard Rothmann was a German preacher who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in the city of Münster, where he won much of the citizenry to Lutheran views by 1532. He then rejected infant baptism and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a>, becoming the chief writer of the Anabaptist kingdom of Münster under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-matthys">Jan Matthys</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-leiden">Jan of Leiden</a>. He is thought to have died when the city fell in June 1535.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Rothmann was born at Stadtlohn in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>. In the late 1520s he became the leader of religious reform in Münster, preaching against purgatory, the use of images and the low morals of the priests. Censured by the Catholic bishop in 1531, he denied the authority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>, published an evangelical creed in January 1532 and won the backing of the city authorities. By the treaty of 14 February 1533 Münster was recognised as a Lutheran city.</p><p>In the summer of 1533 the Anabaptist disciples of Melchior Hoffman won him over to their rejection of infant baptism, which he preached from his pulpit at St. Lambert's church. The city council censured him, but his popularity with the craft <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> kept him safe. After Hoffman was imprisoned in Strasbourg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-matthys">Jan Matthys</a> declared Münster the New Jerusalem, and Rothmann was baptised on 5 January 1534. Under Matthys, who died in a failed sortie on Easter Sunday 1534, and then under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-leiden">John of Leiden</a> as King of Münster, Rothmann was probably the most important theological voice of the movement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His writings of 1534 were A Confession of Faith and Life in the Church of Christ of Münster, A Restitution of Christian Teaching, Faith, and Life in October and Concerning Revenge in December. Unlike many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a> of his time he held immersion to be the proper mode of baptism, arguing from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> words doepen and dumpelen, from the Scriptural passages that describe baptism as a burial or a washing of the body, and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tertullian">Tertullian</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/origen">Origen</a> and Gratian's Decretum.</p><p>He accepted the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> as the word of God, held the celestial flesh view of Christ taught by Kaspar Schwenkfeld and Hoffman, and saw the church as a gathering of baptised believers only. He first opposed the polygamy that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-leiden">John of Leiden</a> introduced, then wrote in its defence, leaning on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rothmann may have died fighting when Münster was retaken in June 1535, or may have escaped in the turmoil. His body was not identified, he was apparently not among the small band of survivors around Heinrich Krechting that contemporary sources attest, and he was never heard from again. Pilgram Marpeck borrowed part of his Vermanung from Rothmann's Bekenntnisse of 1533, carrying his influence to the south German Anabaptists, and his view of the incarnation prevailed among Dutch Anabaptists in their first century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bernhard Rothmann” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Münster, capital of the Prince-Bishopric of Münster, had turned Lutheran under the preacher <b>Bernhard Rothmann</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/munster-rebellion">Münster Rebellion</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Stadtlohn, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Münster, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.99,6.92&amp;year=1521" aria-label="Stadtlohn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Bernhard Rothmann"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5363 5664h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5363 5664h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.99,6.92&amp;year=1521">What was here in 1521? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
